{info:title=Keystore Management with HashiCorp Vault}
This guide covers the implementation of centralized keystore management using Vault's Key/Value (KV) secrets engine for secure storage, versioning, and access control of sensitive configuration data.
{info}

{panel:title=Page Information|borderStyle=dashed|borderColor=#ccc|titleBGColor=#d9ead3|bgColor=#f9f9f9}
*Target Audience:* Platform Engineers, DevOps Engineers, Security Engineers
*Estimated Reading Time:* 15-20 minutes
*Prerequisites:* Basic understanding of HashiCorp Vault, administrative access to Vault instance
*Related Pages:* [Vault Cloud Setup Guide|02-vault-cloud-setup-guide], [Kubernetes Vault Integration|06-kubernetes-vault-integration]
{panel}

h1. Table of Contents

{toc:printable=true|style=square|maxLevel=3|indent=10px|minLevel=1|exclude=Table of Contents}

h1. Overview

Keystore management in HashiCorp Vault involves securely storing, versioning, and controlling access to sensitive configuration data such as API keys, passwords, certificates, and other secrets. This guide covers the implementation of centralized keystore management using Vault's Key/Value (KV) secrets engine.

h2. Benefits of Vault Keystore Management

{panel:title=Key Benefits|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#34495e|titleBGColor=#3498db|titleColor=#ffffff|bgColor=#f8f9fa}
• *Centralized Storage:* Single source of truth for all secrets
• *Access Control:* Fine-grained permissions and audit trails  
• *Versioning:* Track changes and rollback capabilities
• *Encryption:* Automatic encryption at rest and in transit
• *Compliance:* Comprehensive audit logging for regulatory requirements
{panel}

h1. KV Secrets Engine

h2. 1. Enable KV Secrets Engine

{code:language=bash|title=Enable KV Secrets Engine|borderStyle=solid}
# Enable KV v2 secrets engine
vault secrets enable -version=2 kv

# Enable KV v2 at custom path
vault secrets enable -path=secret -version=2 kv

# Verify secrets engines
vault secrets list
{code}

h2. 2. KV v2 Features

{note:title=KV v2 Features}
The KV v2 secrets engine provides:
• *Versioning:* Multiple versions of each secret
• *Soft Delete:* Secrets can be deleted and recovered
• *Check-and-Set:* Prevent concurrent modifications
• *Metadata:* Additional information about secrets
{note}

h2. 3. Basic Operations

{code:language=bash|title=Basic KV Operations|borderStyle=solid}
# Write a secret
vault kv put secret/myapp/database \
    username=dbuser \
    password=supersecret \
    host=db.example.com \
    port=5432

# Read a secret
vault kv get secret/myapp/database

# List secrets
vault kv list secret/

# List secrets in subdirectory
vault kv list secret/myapp/

# Get secret metadata
vault kv metadata get secret/myapp/database

# Get specific version
vault kv get -version=2 secret/myapp/database
{code}

h1. Organizing Secrets

h2. 1. Namespace Structure

Design a logical namespace structure for different environments and applications:

{noformat}
secret/
├── prod/
│   ├── app1/
│   │   ├── database
│   │   ├── api-keys
│   │   └── certificates
│   ├── app2/
│   │   ├── database
│   │   └── external-services
│   └── shared/
│       ├── monitoring
│       └── backup-credentials
├── staging/
│   ├── app1/
│   └── app2/
├── dev/
│   ├── app1/
│   └── app2/
└── shared/
    ├── ci-cd
    └── infrastructure
{noformat}

h2. 2. Naming Conventions

{panel:title=Naming Convention Examples|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#e67e22|titleBGColor=#f39c12|titleColor=#ffffff}
*Environment-based structure:*
{{secret/{environment}/{application}/{service}}}
{{secret/prod/ecommerce/database}}

*Service-based structure:*
{{secret/{service}/{environment}/{component}}}
{{secret/database/prod/credentials}}

*Team-based structure:*
{{secret/{team}/{environment}/{service}}}
{{secret/platform/prod/monitoring}}
{panel}

h2. 3. Secret Categories

{code:language=bash|title=Secret Organization Examples|borderStyle=solid}
# Database credentials
vault kv put secret/prod/app1/database \
    username=app1_user \
    password=secure_password \
    host=prod-db.internal \
    port=5432 \
    ssl_mode=require

# API keys and tokens
vault kv put secret/prod/app1/api-keys \
    stripe_secret_key=sk_live_... \
    sendgrid_api_key=SG... \
    aws_access_key=AKIA... \
    aws_secret_key=...

# Certificates and keys
vault kv put secret/prod/app1/certificates \
    tls_cert=@/path/to/cert.pem \
    tls_key=@/path/to/key.pem \
    ca_cert=@/path/to/ca.pem

# Application configuration
vault kv put secret/prod/app1/config \
    jwt_secret=random_jwt_secret \
    encryption_key=32_byte_encryption_key \
    session_secret=session_signing_key
{code}

h1. Access Control and Policies

h2. 1. Basic Policies

{code:language=hcl|title=Basic Policy Examples|borderStyle=solid}
# Read-only access to production secrets
path "secret/data/prod/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Full access to development secrets
path "secret/data/dev/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

# Metadata access
path "secret/metadata/dev/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list", "delete"]
}
{code}

h2. 2. Environment-Based Policies

{code:language=hcl|title=Environment-Based Access Control|borderStyle=solid}
# Production read-only policy
path "secret/data/prod/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

path "secret/metadata/prod/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}

# Development full access policy
path "secret/data/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

path "secret/metadata/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}
{code}

h2. 3. Application-Specific Policies

{code:language=hcl|title=Application-Specific Policies|borderStyle=solid}
# Policy for web application
path "secret/data/prod/webapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Policy for CI/CD system
path "secret/data/*/webapp/deployment" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Policy for backup service
path "secret/data/shared/backup-credentials" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
{code}

h1. Secret Lifecycle Management

h2. 1. Secret Versioning

{tip:title=Version Management Best Practices}
• Always use KV v2 for versioning capabilities
• Document significant changes in secret metadata
• Implement automated rotation for sensitive credentials
• Maintain version history for compliance requirements
{tip}

{code:language=bash|title=Version Management Commands|borderStyle=solid}
# Create new version of secret
vault kv put secret/myapp/database username=newuser password=newpassword

# View all versions
vault kv metadata get secret/myapp/database

# Get specific version
vault kv get -version=1 secret/myapp/database

# Delete specific version (soft delete)
vault kv delete -versions=1,2 secret/myapp/database

# Permanently delete versions
vault kv destroy -versions=1 secret/myapp/database

# Undelete soft-deleted versions
vault kv undelete -versions=1 secret/myapp/database
{code}

h2. 2. Secret Rotation

{code:language=bash|title=Secret Rotation Procedures|borderStyle=solid}
# Rotation workflow example
# 1. Generate new secret value
NEW_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -base64 32)

# 2. Update secret with new value
vault kv put secret/prod/app1/database \
    username=app1_user \
    password="$NEW_PASSWORD" \
    host=prod-db.internal

# 3. Update application configuration
# 4. Verify application functionality
# 5. Remove old secret version if needed
vault kv destroy -versions=1 secret/prod/app1/database
{code}

h2. 3. Metadata Management

{code:language=bash|title=Metadata Management|borderStyle=solid}
# Set custom metadata
vault kv metadata put secret/myapp/database \
    max_versions=10 \
    cas_required=false

# Set delete protection
vault kv metadata put secret/myapp/database \
    delete_version_after=72h
{code}

h1. Application Integration

h2. 1. Environment Variables Integration

{code:language=bash|title=Environment Variable Integration|borderStyle=solid}
#!/bin/bash
# Script to load secrets as environment variables

# Function to load secrets
load_secrets() {
    local secret_path="$1"
    
    # Get secret data as JSON
    secret_json=$(vault kv get -format=json "$secret_path" | jq -r '.data.data')
    
    # Export each key-value pair
    echo "$secret_json" | jq -r 'to_entries[] | "export \(.key)=\(.value)"' | source
}

# Load application secrets
load_secrets "secret/prod/myapp/database"
load_secrets "secret/prod/myapp/api-keys"

# Start application
exec ./myapp
{code}

h2. 2. Configuration File Generation

{code:language=bash|title=Configuration File Template|borderStyle=solid}
# Template file: config.yaml.tpl
database:
  host: "{{ .host }}"
  port: {{ .port }}
  username: "{{ .username }}"
  password: "{{ .password }}"
  ssl_mode: "{{ .ssl_mode }}"

api_keys:
  stripe: "{{ .stripe_secret_key }}"
  sendgrid: "{{ .sendgrid_api_key }}"

# Script to generate configuration
vault kv get -format=json secret/prod/myapp/database | \
    jq -r '.data.data' | \
    consul-template -template="config.yaml.tpl:config.yaml" -once
{code}

h2. 3. Application Code Examples

{expand:title=Python Integration Example}
{code:language=python|title=Python Vault Client|borderStyle=solid}
import hvac
import os

class VaultClient:
    def __init__(self):
        self.client = hvac.Client(
            url=os.environ['VAULT_ADDR'],
            token=os.environ['VAULT_TOKEN']
        )
        
    def get_secret(self, path):
        """Retrieve secret from Vault KV store"""
        try:
            response = self.client.secrets.kv.v2.read_secret_version(
                path=path
            )
            return response['data']['data']
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"Error retrieving secret {path}: {e}")
            return None
    
    def get_database_config(self, app_name, environment='prod'):
        """Get database configuration for application"""
        secret_path = f"{environment}/{app_name}/database"
        return self.get_secret(secret_path)

# Usage example
vault_client = VaultClient()
db_config = vault_client.get_database_config('myapp', 'prod')

if db_config:
    DATABASE_URL = f"postgresql://{db_config['username']}:{db_config['password']}@{db_config['host']}:{db_config['port']}/myapp"
{code}
{expand}

{expand:title=Go Integration Example}
{code:language=go|title=Go Vault Client|borderStyle=solid}
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "log"
    "os"
    
    vault "github.com/hashicorp/vault/api"
)

type VaultClient struct {
    client *vault.Client
}

func NewVaultClient() (*VaultClient, error) {
    client, err := vault.NewClient(vault.DefaultConfig())
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    
    client.SetToken(os.Getenv("VAULT_TOKEN"))
    
    return &VaultClient{client: client}, nil
}

func (vc *VaultClient) GetSecret(path string) (map[string]interface{}, error) {
    secret, err := vc.client.Logical().Read(fmt.Sprintf("secret/data/%s", path))
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    
    if secret == nil {
        return nil, fmt.Errorf("secret not found at path: %s", path)
    }
    
    data := secret.Data["data"].(map[string]interface{})
    return data, nil
}

func main() {
    vaultClient, err := NewVaultClient()
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    
    dbConfig, err := vaultClient.GetSecret("prod/myapp/database")
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    
    fmt.Printf("Database Host: %s\n", dbConfig["host"])
}
{code}
{expand}

h1. Best Practices

{panel:title=Security Best Practices|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#27ae60|titleBGColor=#2ecc71|titleColor=#ffffff}
h4. Access Control
• Use principle of least privilege for policy design
• Implement time-bound tokens with appropriate TTL
• Regular access reviews and policy audits
• Separate policies for different environments

h4. Secret Management
• Use descriptive names and consistent organization
• Implement regular secret rotation schedules
• Document secret purposes and dependencies
• Use metadata for additional context

h4. Operations
• Monitor secret access patterns and anomalies
• Implement backup and recovery procedures
• Use automation for routine secret management tasks
• Maintain audit trails for compliance
{panel}

h2. 1. Secret Organization Guidelines

{tip:title=Organization Best Practices}
*Hierarchical Structure:* Use consistent path hierarchies across environments
*Naming Standards:* Establish and enforce naming conventions
*Environment Separation:* Separate production from non-production secrets
*Team Boundaries:* Align secret organization with team responsibilities
{tip}

h2. 2. Policy Design Patterns

{code:language=hcl|title=Policy Design Patterns|borderStyle=solid}
# Parameterized policy for multiple environments
path "secret/data/{{identity.entity.metadata.environment}}/{{identity.entity.metadata.team}}/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Time-based access policy
path "secret/data/prod/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
  allowed_parameters = {
    "access_time" = ["09:00-17:00"]
  }
}

# Conditional access based on IP
path "secret/data/prod/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
  bound_cidrs = ["10.0.0.0/8", "192.168.0.0/16"]
}
{code}

h1. Monitoring and Auditing

h2. 1. Audit Configuration

{code:language=bash|title=Audit Logging Setup|borderStyle=solid}
# Enable file audit device
vault audit enable file file_path=/vault/logs/audit.log

# Enable syslog audit device
vault audit enable syslog

# View audit devices
vault audit list
{code}

h2. 2. Monitoring Secret Access

{warning:title=Important Monitoring Metrics}
Monitor these key metrics for secret management:
• Secret access frequency and patterns
• Failed authentication attempts
• Policy violations and denials
• Secret version changes and rotations
• Unusual access times or locations
{warning}

h2. 3. Compliance and Reporting

{panel:title=Compliance Considerations|borderStyle=dashed|borderColor=#9b59b6|titleBGColor=#e8d5f0}
• Maintain audit logs for regulatory requirements
• Document access controls and policy rationale
• Regular compliance assessments and reviews
• Automated reporting for audit purposes
• Data retention policies for audit logs
{panel}

h1. Troubleshooting

h2. Common Issues and Solutions

{expand:title=Permission Denied Errors}
*Problem:* Users cannot access secrets despite having policies assigned.

*Solutions:*
1. Verify policy assignment: {{vault auth list}} and check user's token
2. Check policy syntax: {{vault policy read policy-name}}
3. Verify secret path exists: {{vault kv list secret/path/}}
4. Check namespace context if using Vault Enterprise
{expand}

{expand:title=Secret Not Found}
*Problem:* Secret exists but cannot be retrieved.

*Solutions:*
1. Verify exact path: {{vault kv list}} to check structure
2. Check secret version: {{vault kv metadata get secret/path}}
3. Verify KV engine mount: {{vault secrets list}}
4. Check if secret was soft-deleted: {{vault kv metadata get}}
{expand}

{expand:title=Version Management Issues}
*Problem:* Cannot access specific versions or version limits exceeded.

*Solutions:*
1. Check version history: {{vault kv metadata get secret/path}}
2. Verify max_versions setting in metadata
3. Use {{vault kv undelete}} for soft-deleted versions
4. Adjust metadata settings: {{vault kv metadata put}}
{expand}

h2. Diagnostic Commands

{code:language=bash|title=Diagnostic Commands|borderStyle=solid}
# Check current token capabilities
vault token capabilities secret/data/prod/myapp/database

# Verify policy assignment
vault token lookup

# Check secret metadata
vault kv metadata get secret/prod/myapp/database

# List available secret paths
vault kv list secret/

# Test policy with different paths
vault auth -method=userpass username=testuser
vault kv get secret/test/path
{code}

